You are a researcher in the field of fairness-aware data sampling and you have been provided with an abstract from a paper discussing various experiments conducted to evaluate the performance of different algorithms for constructing \u03b5-nets that satisfy certain fairness constraints. The paragraphs below detail the experimental setup, methodologies, results, and conclusions drawn from these experiments.

The authors have used three datasets: Adult, COMPAS, and College Admission to generate a collection of range queries represented as hyper-rectangles in feature space. They partitioned data into two groups for each attribute using thresholds, resulting in up to 2d hyper-rectangles (where d is the number of features). An \u03b5-net was computed over these partitions to satisfy various fairness constraints under CR conditions. The results are depicted in Figure 4 and show that FMC outperforms baseline sampling methods, particularly when considering unfairness measures like demographic parity (F2) and rank regret.

In the experiments involving synthetic datasets consisting of randomly sampled points and geometric ranges, they varied dimensionality, number of colors, and range counts to test scalability and performance on larger datasets. The results indicate that FMC and Fair Sketch-and-Merge (FSM) methods achieve near-zero unfairness compared to the standard sampling method which shows significantly higher levels of unfairness.

The experiments also included a comparison of running times for different algorithms, with Figure 12 and 13 illustrating that FMC is highly scalable even on larger datasets like PopSim 5, as it ran in only 0.3 seconds for n=1M data points. The authors have provided additional results comparing the output size and runtime of standard sampling and Discrepancy-based methods in their technical report [32].

The experiments also explored Rank Regret Representatives by using real-world datasets to construct an \u03b5-net that intersects with heavy top-lregions for any ranking function defined over the dataset features. The evaluation was done across different values of \u03b5, and results consistent with previous settings were observed.

The technical report [32] provides more detailed information on these experiments.

