The authors have used three datasets: Adult, COMPAS, and College Admission to generate a collection of range queries represented as hyper-rectangles in feature space. They partitioned data into two groups for each attribute using thresholds, resulting in up to 2d hyper-rectangles (where d is the number of features). An \u03b5-net was computed over these partitions to satisfy various fairness constraints under CR conditions. The results are depicted in Figure 4 and show that FMC outperforms baseline sampling methods, achieving near-zero unfairness while introducing only a minimal increase in sample size compared to the standard approach (logarithmic to k). A comparison of the running time between standard Sampling and Fair Monte Carlo (FMC) is provided in the technical report [32]. Figures 12 and 13 show a comparison of FMC and sampling methods on larger datasets, demonstrating that FMC ran efficiently even for n=1M data points.

The experiments also included Rank Regret Representatives using three real-world datasets: Adult, COMPAS, and Admissions. The goal was to construct an \u03b5-net that intersects the heavy top-lregions of any ranking function defined over the dataset features. Results are consistent with previous settings and more details can be found in the technical report [32].

